\details{
\code{configure()} builds a configuration grid — the Cartesian product of all
specified modeling axes. Each row represents a distinct pipeline to
evaluate. This is the bridge between data preparation (\code{add_response()})
and model evaluation (\code{evaluate()}).

\strong{Outcome promotion:}

\code{add_response()} assigns \code{role = "response"} to joined variables.
\code{configure()} promotes exactly one response to \code{role = "outcome"} for
modeling. If only one response exists, it's auto-selected. Multiple
responses require explicit \code{outcome} specification.

\strong{Covariate expansion:}

When covariates are present, \code{expand_covariates} controls whether to
benchmark across different covariate subsets (power-set expansion) or
use all covariates in every configuration.

\strong{Reconfiguration:}

Can be called multiple times on the same object. Previous configuration
is overwritten, any prior outcome role is reverted to "response", and
everything a previous \code{evaluate()}, \code{fit()} or \code{ensemble()} earned is
dropped: the \code{evaluation}, \code{models} and \code{ensemble} slots return to their
empty state, and the \code{horizons_eval}, \code{horizons_fit} or
\code{horizons_ensemble} class goes with them. All of it is keyed to the
outcome that is being replaced. The validation verdict is cleared too,
but the record of outliers \code{validate()} already removed is kept, since
those rows stay removed, and so is a \code{select_training()} record, which
describes the rows rather than the outcome. Because neither was chosen for
the new outcome, \code{configure()} warns when rows were removed as response
outliers of a different outcome, and when a \code{select_training()} record
(other than \code{scope = "global"}) was drawn for properties that do not
include it.
This enables the \code{purrr::map()} multi-outcome pattern:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{c("SOC", "POM_C", "pH") |>
  purrr::map(~base |> configure(outcome = .x) |> evaluate() |> fit())
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Reproducibility:}

\code{evaluate()} and \code{fit()} pin the RNG seed and kind, so a run is
repeatable from its recorded \code{seed} for every model except \code{cubist}.
The Cubist C implementation is not bit-reproducible when
\code{committees > 1}: repeated fits on identical data under an identical
\code{set.seed()}, or under an explicit \code{cubistControl(seed = )}, can differ in
the fourth significant figure of a fold metric (measured on Cubist
0.6.0). \code{committees = 1} is stable. The effect is small, but it can flip
\code{select_best()} between near-tied grid points and move a reported test
metric by a few percent, so a \code{cubist} result should be read as
reproducible to that tolerance rather than exactly. \code{rf} and the other
engines are deterministic given \code{seed}. See GitHub issue #51.

\strong{Choosing models for large spectral libraries:}

Cost scales very differently with predictor count (\code{p}) across
\code{MODEL_SPECS}. \code{rf} (ranger), \code{xgboost}, \code{lightgbm} and \code{elastic_net}
(glmnet) stay fast at full spectral resolution; \code{plsr} is built for
\verb{p >> n} and is not a concern either. \code{cubist} fits a linear model in
every rule, so its cost grows sharply with \code{p}: on the KSSL clay library
at 4 cm-1 (14,228 rows x 851 predictors, ~12.1M cells), not one of 25
tune tasks finished in 29.5 minutes, while the same config with
\code{feature_selection = "pca"} finished in 368 s (test RPD 3.82) — OSSL's
published pipeline is SNV -> PCA(120) -> Cubist (Safanelli et al. 2025,
PLOS ONE 20(1):e0296545). \code{validate()}'s P010 check warns when a
\code{cubist} config with \code{feature_selection = "none"} sits on a table whose
\code{n_rows * n_predictors} exceeds \code{CUBIST_MAX_CELLS} (a conservative floor
well under the measured failure point, not a benchmark); the fix is
\code{feature_selection = "pca"}. \code{svm_rbf} (kernlab) and \code{mars} (earth) also
scale with \code{p} on well-established grounds — an RBF kernel matrix costs
\verb{O(n^2 p)} to build, and MARS's forward pass searches every predictor at
every candidate knot — but neither has been measured at library scale,
and \code{validate()} does not warn for them. A slow run with either on a
full-resolution table is a signal to add \code{feature_selection} there too.
See GitHub issue #40.
